FoodExpress - Opening the Kubernetes Dashboard from your laptop
2025MT03083 / Ajeet Kumar Yadav
Server: sjo-vm-sqala076   (minikube runs here)
==========================================================


STEP 1 - ON THE SERVER: check the dashboard is running
----------------------------------------------------------
kubectl -n kubernetes-dashboard get pods,svc

Expected:
  pod/kubernetes-dashboard-...      1/1   Running
  service/kubernetes-dashboard      ClusterIP   ...   80/TCP

If it isn't there:
  minikube addons enable dashboard
  minikube addons enable metrics-server


STEP 2 - ON THE SERVER: start the port-forward
----------------------------------------------------------
kubectl -n kubernetes-dashboard port-forward --address 0.0.0.0 svc/kubernetes-dashboard 8080:80 &

Expected output:
  Forwarding from 0.0.0.0:8080 -> 9090

Leave this terminal open. If you close it, the port-forward stops.
"jobs" (csh) lists what's still running in the background.
"Address already in use" -> pick another port, e.g. 8081:80.

Find the server's IP (needed if the hostname doesn't resolve on your laptop):
  hostname -I


STEP 3 - ON YOUR LAPTOP: open the dashboard
----------------------------------------------------------
Option A - direct (try this first)
  Open in the browser:
    http://sjo-vm-sqala076:8080
  or, if the name doesn't resolve:
    http://<server-ip>:8080

Option B - SSH tunnel (if Option A times out because of a firewall)
  Keep the Step 2 port-forward running on the server, then on your laptop
  (Windows PowerShell / macOS / Linux terminal):
    ssh -L 8080:localhost:8080 yadava@sjo-vm-sqala076
  Leave that SSH session open, then open in the browser:
    http://localhost:8080

If a login/token page appears, click "Skip".


STEP 4 - IN THE DASHBOARD
----------------------------------------------------------
1. Namespace dropdown (top left) -> choose "foodexpress"
   (the default is "default", which looks empty)
2. CPU/memory graphs can take 1-2 minutes to appear after metrics-server starts.


